by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T15:42:28Z

Thanks for the rework, the four bugs and the four naming points are all settled. I pushed one change on top and rebased on 8.2.

The cooldown was invisible where users look. With the option on and every newer version inside the window, `getAvailableUpdates()` dropped the package, so `importmap:outdated` printed "No updates found." while an update was being deliberately held back. The only trace was a warning on the `asset_mapper` monolog channel, which the console never shows. In the partial case the command reported the eligible version in the `Latest` column with nothing to say it was not the registry's latest.

So the checker now keeps the package in its result and records the held back version in a new `PackageUpdateInfo::$withheldVersion`, and `importmap:outdated` renders a `Withheld` column plus a matching `withheld` key in `--format=json`. Both only appear when the option is enabled, so output is unchanged for projects that leave it off. A package whose only newer version is withheld is listed with `Latest` equal to `Current` and does not make the command exit with a failure, since there is nothing to act on. `importmap:update` behaves exactly as before, because such an entry reports no update.

I tried this with the existing API first: there was no way for the command to learn that a version had been held back, which is what justifies the new property.

Also in this push: the warning message no longer says "Skipping package", since the package is no longer skipped; the `minimum_release_age` info text now mentions that enabling it makes update checks download the full npm metadata document; the changelog gained the `$logger` argument and the new property; the two commits are squashed into one with your authorship.

The description is rewritten as a first draft for the documentation, with the selection rules, the public API and the traps at the end.
